1
00:00:02,545 --> 00:00:04,172
I have a special treat
for y'all today.

2
00:00:04,380 --> 00:00:06,716
My dear friend and former
college roommate is here

3
00:00:06,924 --> 00:00:08,134
from the Johnson space center

4
00:00:08,342 --> 00:00:11,137
in Houston to talk to us
about our space program.

5
00:00:11,345 --> 00:00:13,306
And yeah, I know
what you're thinking.

6
00:00:13,514 --> 00:00:15,057
These guys were roomies.

7
00:00:15,600 --> 00:00:16,225
How does one go on

8
00:00:16,434 --> 00:00:19,020
to be a...
A fancy scientist at NASA,

9
00:00:19,270 --> 00:00:21,439
and the other's teaching
freshman science

10
00:00:21,647 --> 00:00:23,024
at a public high school?

11
00:00:24,108 --> 00:00:25,108
Yeah, Sheldon.

12
00:00:25,234 --> 00:00:26,819
I was thinking that.

13
00:00:27,904 --> 00:00:28,904
Thank you.

14
00:00:30,239 --> 00:00:31,799
All right, uh, let's
give a warm welcome

15
00:00:31,908 --> 00:00:35,453
to my good buddy,
Dr. Ronald Hodges.

16
00:00:36,662 --> 00:00:37,455
Hey, kids. Hey.

17
00:00:37,663 --> 00:00:39,790
Glad to be here.
And, uh, Hubert, the answer

18
00:00:39,999 --> 00:00:43,711
to your question is, one of
those roomies was busy studying

19
00:00:43,920 --> 00:00:45,922
while the other was out
chasing high school girls.

20
00:00:48,424 --> 00:00:49,424
Yeah.

21
00:00:49,592 --> 00:00:51,802
To be clear, they
were all over 18.

22
00:00:52,011 --> 00:00:53,262
Yeah. Well...

23
00:00:54,263 --> 00:00:56,682
Now, what I thought would
be fun to talk about today

24
00:00:56,891 --> 00:01:00,269
is what NASA's planning on doing
beyond the space shuttle.

25
00:01:00,478 --> 00:01:04,065
Things like the first
manned mission to Mars.

26
00:01:04,565 --> 00:01:05,316
Uh, yeah.

27
00:01:05,525 --> 00:01:07,109
Did you see the movie aliens?

28
00:01:07,777 --> 00:01:08,569
I did.

29
00:01:08,778 --> 00:01:10,238
Did you think it was cool?

30
00:01:11,697 --> 00:01:12,697
Uh...

31
00:01:13,616 --> 00:01:14,909
Sure, I enjoyed it.

32
00:01:15,201 --> 00:01:16,202
Me, too.

33
00:01:17,119 --> 00:01:18,371
Football player, right?

34
00:01:18,663 --> 00:01:19,663
Yes, sir.

35
00:01:19,789 --> 00:01:20,957
Yeah.

36
00:01:21,415 --> 00:01:23,417
Now, of course, before

37
00:01:23,626 --> 00:01:25,962
we can set out
to explore the solar system,

38
00:01:26,170 --> 00:01:28,297
we've got a few minor problems
to overcome.

39
00:01:28,506 --> 00:01:30,216
Not the least of
which is, every time

40
00:01:30,508 --> 00:01:32,593
we launch,
it costs the U.S. taxpayers

41
00:01:32,802 --> 00:01:34,971
hundreds of millions of dollars.

42
00:01:35,805 --> 00:01:36,805
Oh, no, no, no.

43
00:01:37,265 --> 00:01:39,016
- Yes.
- If you want to save money,

44
00:01:39,225 --> 00:01:40,705
why don't you land
the booster rockets

45
00:01:40,768 --> 00:01:42,979
instead of letting them drop
in the ocean?

46
00:01:44,981 --> 00:01:48,568
That's a cute idea, but, uh,
it's not technically possible.

47
00:01:48,818 --> 00:01:49,902
Why not?

48
00:01:51,862 --> 00:01:53,739
Well, it's hard to explain.

49
00:01:53,948 --> 00:01:55,324
The math is pretty complicated.

50
00:01:55,533 --> 00:01:57,159
Perhaps
I could help you with it.

51
00:01:57,827 --> 00:01:58,869
Well,

52
00:01:59,078 --> 00:02:00,913
I bet you could.

53
00:02:01,122 --> 00:02:02,748
I'll bet you could.

54
00:02:02,957 --> 00:02:06,252
I'll tell you what.
Here is a NASA patch.

55
00:02:06,460 --> 00:02:09,797
Why don't you have your mom
sew that on your book bag? Now,

56
00:02:10,214 --> 00:02:12,633
who here wants to know how
astronauts go to the bathroom?

57
00:02:12,842 --> 00:02:14,202
- Boy: -Me. I do.
- Oh, right. See,

58
00:02:14,302 --> 00:02:16,345
now, during liftoff,
all the astronauts

59
00:02:16,554 --> 00:02:17,555
wear diapers.

60
00:02:17,847 --> 00:02:19,849
Adult Sheldon:
People often wonder why I chose

61
00:02:20,057 --> 00:02:23,269
to pursue a career
in theoretical physics.

62
00:02:23,853 --> 00:02:25,646
I usually respond by saying

63
00:02:25,855 --> 00:02:28,816
I wanted to unravel the
inner workings of the universe.

64
00:02:29,025 --> 00:02:29,692
Yeah, perhaps when

65
00:02:29,900 --> 00:02:32,194
captain kirk said,
"to boldly go,"

66
00:02:32,403 --> 00:02:34,614
he meant in one of those, huh?

67
00:02:34,822 --> 00:02:36,407
Adult Sheldon:
But the real answer was

68
00:02:36,616 --> 00:02:38,701
I wanted
to prove this nincompoop wrong.

69
00:02:38,909 --> 00:02:40,620
Yeah, that's right.
Beam me up, potty!

70
00:02:42,413 --> 00:02:45,207
J” nobody else
is stronger than I am j”

71
00:02:45,416 --> 00:02:48,002
j" yesterday I moved a mountain j”

72
00:02:48,210 --> 00:02:50,671
j" I bet I could be your hero j”

73
00:02:50,880 --> 00:02:53,466
j" I am a mighty little man j“

74
00:02:53,716 --> 00:02:56,969
j" I am a mighty little man. J“

75
00:03:00,598 --> 00:03:02,391
meemaw: So I saw a lawyer today.

76
00:03:02,808 --> 00:03:03,434
Why?

77
00:03:03,643 --> 00:03:05,936
I'm putting together
my last will and testament.

78
00:03:06,395 --> 00:03:07,395
We're gonna miss ya.

79
00:03:07,688 --> 00:03:08,314
George.

80
00:03:08,522 --> 00:03:10,858
Don't worry.
I ain't leaving him squat.

81
00:03:11,359 --> 00:03:12,359
Are you dyin'?

82
00:03:12,443 --> 00:03:13,611
We're all dyin', honey.

83
00:03:13,819 --> 00:03:14,899
From the second we're born,

84
00:03:14,945 --> 00:03:16,947
it's just a slip and slide
into the darkness.

85
00:03:17,239 --> 00:03:19,033
Unless you get bit by a vampire.

86
00:03:19,408 --> 00:03:22,119
Well, now that goes
without saying.

87
00:03:24,163 --> 00:03:25,599
I don't want to die.
I've only kissed

88
00:03:25,623 --> 00:03:26,957
one boy so far.

89
00:03:27,249 --> 00:03:28,249
What?

90
00:03:28,376 --> 00:03:30,378
Relax. It was a long time ago.

91
00:03:31,837 --> 00:03:32,922
Are you relaxed?

92
00:03:33,756 --> 00:03:34,423
Shelly, you haven't

93
00:03:34,632 --> 00:03:35,966
touched your dinner.

94
00:03:36,258 --> 00:03:37,301
You feel okay?

95
00:03:37,885 --> 00:03:40,012
Oh, yes. I was just thinking.

96
00:03:40,596 --> 00:03:41,596
About what, baby?

97
00:03:41,847 --> 00:03:45,184
The optimal height-to-width
ratio for a reusable rocket.

98
00:03:46,143 --> 00:03:46,769
Meemaw: I was just

99
00:03:46,977 --> 00:03:48,646
thinking the exact same thing.

100
00:03:49,188 --> 00:03:50,272
Really?

101
00:03:50,690 --> 00:03:52,400
Oh, moonpie, I love you so much.

102
00:04:15,840 --> 00:04:17,120
It's late. What
are you doing up?

103
00:04:17,174 --> 00:04:19,093
I'm working
on the hyperbolic calculations

104
00:04:19,301 --> 00:04:20,344
for a rocket return.

105
00:04:21,220 --> 00:04:22,220
Need any help?

106
00:04:23,431 --> 00:04:24,431
What?

107
00:04:24,515 --> 00:04:27,643
I'm kiddin'. Now, turn out
the light and go to bed.

108
00:04:28,018 --> 00:04:29,061
- But...
- Now.

109
00:04:34,525 --> 00:04:36,444
Dad, can we afford a computer?

110
00:04:37,027 --> 00:04:39,071
You do my taxes.
What do you think?

111
00:04:39,780 --> 00:04:41,115
Never mind.

112
00:04:41,782 --> 00:04:43,075
Good night.

113
00:05:13,773 --> 00:05:14,774
Meemaw?

114
00:05:17,818 --> 00:05:20,321
Mr. Moonpie,
how might I help you?

115
00:05:20,613 --> 00:05:23,199
How much money are you planning
to leave me when you die?

116
00:05:23,407 --> 00:05:24,533
Nice talkin' to you.

117
00:05:26,660 --> 00:05:28,162
Meemaw?

118
00:05:29,914 --> 00:05:31,290
Want to try that again?

119
00:05:31,499 --> 00:05:32,500
I need to buy a computer

120
00:05:32,708 --> 00:05:34,752
so I can solve
the navier-Stokes equation.

121
00:05:34,960 --> 00:05:37,797
Uh-huh. How much is a computer?

122
00:05:38,005 --> 00:05:40,174
Apple has a nice one for $2,000,

123
00:05:40,382 --> 00:05:42,635
but the kind I need costs
three million.

124
00:05:43,928 --> 00:05:45,471
Let me see
what I got in the vault.

125
00:05:50,184 --> 00:05:53,229
Nine bucks and, uh...

126
00:05:56,065 --> 00:05:58,651
Oh, look at that... a peso.

127
00:06:00,694 --> 00:06:01,821
It's warm.

128
00:06:09,411 --> 00:06:10,663
Hello, first national bank.

129
00:06:10,871 --> 00:06:12,832
I'd like to speak
with a loan officer.

130
00:06:13,040 --> 00:06:15,668
It's regarding a second mortgage
on my home.

131
00:06:16,335 --> 00:06:18,337
My name is Sheldon Lee Cooper.

132
00:06:19,171 --> 00:06:20,256
Sure, I'll hold.

133
00:06:21,382 --> 00:06:23,592
Oh, I hate hold music.

134
00:06:23,926 --> 00:06:24,926
Hello.

135
00:06:25,010 --> 00:06:27,555
Yes, you can help me,
Dorothy Fitzpatrick.

136
00:06:27,930 --> 00:06:30,683
I'm interested in taking out
a second mortgage.

137
00:06:31,559 --> 00:06:33,769
I'm nine years old.
Why do you ask?

138
00:06:34,687 --> 00:06:36,939
That's called
age discrimination, Dorothy,

139
00:06:37,147 --> 00:06:39,358
but I'm willing to let it slide.

140
00:06:40,609 --> 00:06:43,612
I'm glad you find me cute,
but I'm deadly serious.

141
00:06:43,946 --> 00:06:46,240
I need funds to buy a computer.

142
00:06:46,490 --> 00:06:48,033
No, the house isn't in my name.

143
00:06:48,242 --> 00:06:50,327
I'm nine.
We've established this.

144
00:06:51,120 --> 00:06:53,330
I do prepare the taxes
for my parents,

145
00:06:53,539 --> 00:06:54,748
and if we tighten our belts,

146
00:06:54,957 --> 00:06:56,834
we'll have sufficient equity
for the loan.

147
00:06:57,042 --> 00:06:58,335
Sheldon, who are you talking to?

148
00:06:59,044 --> 00:07:00,546
Dorothy Fitzpatrick.

149
00:07:00,796 --> 00:07:01,922
Who's Dorothy Fitzpatrick?

150
00:07:02,131 --> 00:07:03,757
Is she a new buddy from school?

151
00:07:04,008 --> 00:07:06,927
She's the loan officer at the
first national bank of medford.

152
00:07:07,136 --> 00:07:08,220
Why are you talking to her?

153
00:07:08,804 --> 00:07:09,804
Mom, can this wait?

154
00:07:09,889 --> 00:07:12,016
I'm trying
to negotiate favorable terms.

155
00:07:12,600 --> 00:07:14,101
Hello. Dorothy?

156
00:07:19,732 --> 00:07:21,859
Hey, how come you
weren't in math class?

157
00:07:22,067 --> 00:07:24,486
That guy from NASA
treated me like a child,

158
00:07:24,695 --> 00:07:26,739
and I need to prove him wrong.

159
00:07:26,989 --> 00:07:27,989
You are a child.

160
00:07:28,073 --> 00:07:29,825
Tread lightly, my friend.

161
00:07:30,910 --> 00:07:32,870
Was Ms. Ingram upset
I wasn't in class?

162
00:07:33,078 --> 00:07:35,122
Tam: Actually, she was happy.

163
00:07:35,331 --> 00:07:36,624
She even did a little dance.

164
00:07:36,957 --> 00:07:38,542
Do you know anybody
with a modem?

165
00:07:38,751 --> 00:07:40,878
I think
radio shack has one. Why?

166
00:07:41,086 --> 00:07:43,422
I need to access
thrust-to-weight ratios

167
00:07:43,631 --> 00:07:46,342
from the ftp server at
the Johnson space center.

168
00:07:46,675 --> 00:07:47,384
Then what?

169
00:07:47,676 --> 00:07:49,261
Then I show that guy
from NASA my work

170
00:07:49,470 --> 00:07:51,680
and laugh as he begs for mercy.

171
00:07:52,306 --> 00:07:54,516
You know, sometimes
you sound like a super villain.

172
00:07:54,725 --> 00:07:55,768
Silence!

173
00:07:56,477 --> 00:07:57,227
That'll be more effective after

174
00:07:57,436 --> 00:07:58,812
your voice changes.

175
00:08:01,941 --> 00:08:03,359
Silence.

176
00:08:04,735 --> 00:08:07,029
Mom, can you take me
to radio shack?

177
00:08:07,321 --> 00:08:08,322
Not today, baby.

178
00:08:08,530 --> 00:08:11,116
I have to go food shopping
and get dinner started.

179
00:08:11,367 --> 00:08:13,160
But it's important.

180
00:08:13,744 --> 00:08:15,663
Sorry. Maybe over the weekend.

181
00:08:15,955 --> 00:08:17,075
But it won't take that long.

182
00:08:17,164 --> 00:08:18,624
Sheldon, I said no.

183
00:08:18,832 --> 00:08:21,418
- But, mom...
- Not another word.

184
00:08:23,337 --> 00:08:24,337
What?

185
00:08:24,755 --> 00:08:26,131
Can't I just be happy?

186
00:08:29,927 --> 00:08:31,095
Mom, please!

187
00:08:31,303 --> 00:08:33,097
Sheldon Lee, I said no.

188
00:08:33,305 --> 00:08:34,473
I'm still happy.

189
00:08:37,393 --> 00:08:39,103
Please, mom!
You're being unfair!

190
00:08:39,311 --> 00:08:41,939
- No.
- But I really need those thrust-to-weight ratios.

191
00:08:42,147 --> 00:08:43,524
Do you see I'm cookin'?

192
00:08:43,816 --> 00:08:45,067
Well, take me after dinner.

193
00:08:45,275 --> 00:08:47,736
Enough! I'm not takin' you
anywhere tonight.

194
00:08:49,196 --> 00:08:50,340
Adult Sheldon:
I'm not proud of it,

195
00:08:50,364 --> 00:08:52,908
but as a child, I was prone...

196
00:08:53,158 --> 00:08:54,660
To the occasional meltdown.

197
00:08:54,868 --> 00:08:56,537
Sheldon: Cheese and crackers!

198
00:08:56,870 --> 00:08:59,289
Adult Sheldon:
I'm sorry you had to hear that.

199
00:09:04,044 --> 00:09:07,006
All right,
I finally got him calmed down.

200
00:09:07,214 --> 00:09:08,507
How'd you manage that?

201
00:09:08,716 --> 00:09:11,719
Easy. Gave him a hug
and a little cough syrup.

202
00:09:11,927 --> 00:09:12,594
Mom!

203
00:09:12,803 --> 00:09:15,055
It's not like he's
operating heavy machinery.

204
00:09:15,472 --> 00:09:17,641
We can't keep putting
up with this behavior.

205
00:09:18,142 --> 00:09:18,934
We need to ground him.

206
00:09:19,143 --> 00:09:22,271
How? If you say "no playing
outside," he says, "thank you."

207
00:09:22,479 --> 00:09:23,689
Well, we need to do something.

208
00:09:23,897 --> 00:09:26,191
It's just a tantrum.
It's what kids do.

209
00:09:26,400 --> 00:09:27,760
You used to punish me
all the time.

210
00:09:27,943 --> 00:09:30,195
That was different.
You were a pain in the ass.

211
00:09:31,155 --> 00:09:33,115
I say no radio shack
for at least a month.

212
00:09:33,866 --> 00:09:34,575
Sounds fair.

213
00:09:34,783 --> 00:09:35,783
I'll go tell him.

214
00:09:35,951 --> 00:09:37,453
I'd wait and tell him tomorrow.

215
00:09:37,745 --> 00:09:39,329
He's a little loopy right now.

216
00:09:44,001 --> 00:09:45,210
Good night.

217
00:09:46,128 --> 00:09:47,296
She drugged our son.

218
00:09:48,672 --> 00:09:51,842
Adult Sheldon: Thus
began the longest month of my life.

219
00:09:54,053 --> 00:09:57,598
Without the ability to access
the thrust-to-weight ratios,

220
00:09:57,806 --> 00:10:00,976
my well-intended effort
to humiliate a NASA scientist

221
00:10:01,185 --> 00:10:02,269
ground to a halt.

222
00:10:02,478 --> 00:10:03,478
Mom.

223
00:10:03,562 --> 00:10:05,355
Uh... what's going on?

224
00:10:05,814 --> 00:10:07,608
Something's wrong with Sheldon.

225
00:10:09,777 --> 00:10:12,321
What's the matter, baby?
Have a tummy ache?

226
00:10:12,529 --> 00:10:13,822
I think it's an ulcer.

227
00:10:14,198 --> 00:10:14,907
George sr.: Don't be silly.

228
00:10:15,115 --> 00:10:16,825
- You must have eaten something.
- No.

229
00:10:17,034 --> 00:10:19,703
My symptoms are consistent
with an ulcer.

230
00:10:20,120 --> 00:10:21,400
Shelly, remember
when you thought

231
00:10:21,497 --> 00:10:24,208
you had leprosy, and it was
just a patch of dry skin?

232
00:10:24,416 --> 00:10:27,211
And when you thought you had
gout? Or an enlarged prostate?

233
00:10:27,419 --> 00:10:28,420
Or mad cow?

234
00:10:28,629 --> 00:10:29,880
That was my favorite.

235
00:10:30,089 --> 00:10:32,007
Well, this is different.

236
00:10:33,092 --> 00:10:34,843
Maybe we should take
him to the doctor.

237
00:10:36,595 --> 00:10:39,098
Listen to your wife...
Ulcers are serious.

238
00:10:39,389 --> 00:10:40,389
Ow.

239
00:10:40,766 --> 00:10:43,310
Well, I've never seen this
in someone so young,

240
00:10:43,602 --> 00:10:46,522
but, uh, your son has an ulcer.

241
00:10:46,939 --> 00:10:47,939
Thank you.

242
00:10:47,981 --> 00:10:49,233
How could this happen?

243
00:10:49,483 --> 00:10:51,527
Uh, there are
a number of factors.

244
00:10:52,027 --> 00:10:52,778
Has he been under

245
00:10:52,986 --> 00:10:55,072
some unusual stress lately?

246
00:10:55,280 --> 00:10:57,533
Yes. She won't take me
to radio shack.

247
00:10:57,741 --> 00:10:58,951
Go wait outside.

248
00:10:59,201 --> 00:11:00,619
I've heard
some interesting research

249
00:11:00,828 --> 00:11:02,079
about treating ulcers

250
00:11:02,287 --> 00:11:03,287
with antimicrobials.

251
00:11:03,455 --> 00:11:04,455
Go.

252
00:11:06,291 --> 00:11:07,835
And you're surprised
I have an ulcer.

253
00:11:08,043 --> 00:11:09,378
I'm surprised I don't have one.

254
00:11:11,964 --> 00:11:13,382
Um, radio shack?

255
00:11:13,799 --> 00:11:16,802
He's trying to figure out
how to help NASA land rockets.

256
00:11:17,010 --> 00:11:18,720
Oh. Well, that's a nice thing.

257
00:11:18,929 --> 00:11:20,722
He's just doing it out of spite.

258
00:11:21,974 --> 00:11:23,142
Okay.

259
00:11:23,475 --> 00:11:25,602
Well, keep him off spicy
foods for the time being,

260
00:11:25,811 --> 00:11:28,438
and I'll write you
a prescription for zantac.

261
00:11:28,814 --> 00:11:29,814
Does he smoke?

262
00:11:30,399 --> 00:11:31,900
'Course not. He's nine.

